; ============================================================================================
; BT-9b. SIGNAGE, LIT -- the level's materials answer to night legibility. (2026-08-28.)
;
; WHAT THIS IS FOR. BT-8 found that an unshaded `Label3D` renders near-black at midnight, and
; that rebuilding the bubble counter as an emissive `TextMesh` (energy 40, after 3 and 12 both
; measured too dim) still reads at only 8 m against a required 20. BT-8's packet asked for a
; MATERIALS answer so that each widget stops inventing its own. This file and its partner
; `label_backing.tres` are that answer. Apply the pair; neither works alone.
;
; THE RULE THIS OBEYS, AND IT IS A DIRECTED ONE (/direct 2026-08-28, THRILL-BIBLE Part II).
; Programme criterion D10 says the darkness is real and only the named aids get to glow, so a
; self-lit surface needs a licence. The licence is this test, and it is checkable rather than
; a matter of taste:
;
;     A self-lit surface may report the INSTRUMENT'S OWN STATE. It may never reveal the WORLD.
;     The test: does switching it off change what a player can TRAVERSE? If no, it is signage
;     and it is exempt. If yes, it is an aid and it belongs to D10's budget.
;
; A bubble counter and a section label pass that test -- they tell you your score and where you
; are standing, and a player who cannot read either can still cross every platform in the level.
; A lamp over the next jump fails it. This file is for the first kind only.
;
; WHY UNSHADED AND NOT EMISSIVE, WHICH IS THE ACTUAL FIX. An emissive material is still a
; material in a lit scene: it is added to a lambert term that collapses to the night ambient
; floor, so its legibility is a RATIO against whatever the lighting happens to be doing, and
; tuning it is chasing a moving number -- which is exactly what BT-8 was doing at energy 3, 12
; and then 40. An UNSHADED material is not lit at all: Godot writes ALBEDO to the frame directly
; and the result is byte-identical at noon and at midnight. That converts night legibility from
; a tuning problem into an invariant, which is the only form of the answer worth shipping.
;
; NOTE THE TRAP THIS FILE IS DELIBERATELY ON THE RIGHT SIDE OF: an unshaded material IGNORES
; EMISSION. The brightness therefore lives in `albedo_color` and there is no emission block here
; at all. Adding one would look like a fix and do nothing.
;
; THE COST OF UNSHADED, STATED HONESTLY: this surface cannot bloom, because glow is driven from
; emitted luminance and there is none. That is a deliberate trade. Legibility at 20 m is a
; CONTRAST problem, not a brightness problem -- see `label_backing.tres`, which is the half that
; actually buys the distance -- and a blooming sign would also be a light the room did not
; authorise.
; ============================================================================================
;
; Near-white, faintly cool so it does not read as a warm light source in a level whose one warm
; surface is the hub counter (`hub_goal.tres`). L* = 96.41. Paired against `label_backing.tres`
; (L* = 4.02) this is a contrast ratio of 17.6:1 -- past WCAG AAA's 7:1 -- and because BOTH
; materials are unshaded that ratio is CONSTANT at every phase of the cycle.
[gd_resource type="StandardMaterial3D" format=3]

[resource]
resource_name = "label_lit (BT-9b signage)"
shading_mode = 0
albedo_color = Color(0.950, 0.960, 0.980, 1)
cull_mode = 2
